AIM
To write a C program to sort an array of elements using the Bubble Sort algorithm and analyze its time and space complexity using Big-O notation
ALGORITHM
1. Start.
2. Read the number of elements and array elements.
3. Compare each pair of adjacent elements.
4. If the first element is greater than the second, swap them.
5. Repeat the process until the array is sorted.
6. Display the sorted array.
7. Analyze the time and space complexity.
8. Stop.
PSEUDOCODE
Algorithm BubbleSort(A, n)

    for i = 0 to n - 2 do
        for j = 0 to n - i - 2 do
            if A[j] > A[j + 1] then
                swap A[j], A[j + 1]
            end if
        end for
    end for

    print A

End Algorithm
SOURCE CODE (C)

#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a[100], n, i, j, temp;

    printf("Enter number of elements: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    printf("Enter array elements: ");
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        scanf("%d", &a[i]);
    }

    for (i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {
                temp = a[j];
                a[j] = a[j + 1];
                a[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
BIG-O COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS
Case	Time Complexity	Explanation
Best Case	\(O(n^2)\)	In this basic implementation, comparisons continue even if the array is already sorted.
Average Case	\(O(n^2)\)	Multiple comparisons and swaps may be required.
Worst Case	\(O(n^2)\)	A reverse-sorted array requires the maximum number of comparisons and swaps.
Auxiliary Space	\(O(1)\)	Only a temporary variable is needed for swapping.
Note: An optimized Bubble Sort with a swap flag has a best-case time complexity of \(O(n)\) when the array is already sorted.
 RESULT
Thus, the C program successfully sorts the given array using Bubble Sort. The basic implementation has \(O(n^2)\) time complexity and \(O(1)\) auxiliary space complexity.
